WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:09.709
As shown in the previous videos, now we have
a really efficient method of doing multiple

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:15.120
pattern matching using the Burrows-Wheeler
transform. But finding exact pattern matches

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.540
might not be able to answer our question to
identify variants when compared to the reference

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:24.730
genome. So we need to look for inexact matching
in order to find these variants.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:31.210
In general, there 2 methods for identification
of variants. One is called the seeding method

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:35.730
and another is the Burrows-Wheeler approach
as described previously.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:40.000
In the seeding method, assuming we have a
pattern appears in a genome with exactly one

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.890
mismatch, if we divide the pattern into two
pieces, we will notice that one side is going

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:49.870
to match the genome exactly and another side
of the partition has the mismatch.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:54.400
In general, if we have a pattern that occurs
in the genome with d mismatches, then we are

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.629
going to be able to divide that pattern into
d + 1, approximately equal pieces and then

00:00:59.629 --> 00:01:05.100
we are going to be able to find at least one
of those pieces that matches the genome exactly.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:10.770
For example, here is a string of 35 “Xs”.
Assume that we have 6 mismatches with the

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:16.060
reference. If you partition this into seven
pieces with equal length, we will have at

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:21.210
least one partition that have an exact match
with the genome. Hence, whenever we have a

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:26.319
d mismatches, if we divide this pattern into
d plus one pieces, we will always have at

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:30.399
least one partition that has exact match with
the genome.

00:01:30.399 --> 00:01:35.270
This is the principle of the seeding method.
If we are looking for d mismatches, we can

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:40.539
divide each of the pattern strings into d
+ 1 smaller pieces and these pieces are called

00:01:40.539 --> 00:01:46.429
seeds. And then we look for exact matches
of those seeds with our genome. Once we find

00:01:46.429 --> 00:01:51.659
those exact matches, it narrows down what
we have to consider. So we can throw out a

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:57.259
lot of patterns and then look for inexact
matches based on those seed matches.

00:01:57.259 --> 00:02:02.639
Another way to identify the variants would
be to integrate a direct Burrows-Wheeler approach.

00:02:02.639 --> 00:02:06.829
Recall that when we looked at pattern matching
before with Burrows-Wheeler, we search for

00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:12.170
“ANA” in “PANAMABANANAS”. We started
with finding the “A” and we walked backwards

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:17.450
to the other column of the Burrows-Wheeler
matrix and we could find three “Ns”. We

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:22.720
had an “A” and then an “N” and we
would immediately eliminate the “M”, the

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:28.440
“P” and the “B” because they didn't
match the “N” that we were looking for.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.470
If we were allowing one mismatch, then we
need to keep these red letters around. We

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:37.560
need to hold on to them because it may be
the case that we can continue extending and

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:43.740
get exact matches everywhere else and then
we would have an inexact match with one mismatch.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.740
What we'll need to do next is to keep track
of the number of mismatches that we've encountered

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:53.300
along the way. Here, we've encountered zero
mismatches when we've matched these “Ns”

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:58.490
because we have two matched symbols. Here,
we've encountered one mismatched set, the

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:03.480
“M”, the “P” and the “B”. Because
we haven't exceeded one mismatch, we'll need

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.130
to extend all these strings.
We'll use the first last property and take

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:12.100
all of the pattern here and find where each
of these elements occurs back in the first

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:17.650
column. We know that the “A” is going
to be there next. So we carry the mismatches

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:22.820
down and we have more mismatches here. So
we need to eliminate this.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:28.920
Next, we need to identify where those mismatches
are located. We will use a suffix array to

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:35.170
identify where the inexact matches occurred.
We find that there is 1 mismatch in position

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:42.890
5 and position 3, while position 1, 7 and
9 give us the exact matches. With the huge

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:47.400
arsenal of power using Burrows−Wheeler,
we're able to easily step forward and deal

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.030
with inexact matches now.

